    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/BIMSTEC-maritime-Sri-Lanka-and-the-missing-link-in-policy-thinking/4-797176",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1789329260.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailyftlk-7e1f12b5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7b7069208acdc2d5d4dba10af998c427",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1722,
    "chunk_text": "BIMSTEC, maritime Sri Lanka and the missing link in policy thinking\n\nBay of Bengal Initiative for Multi-Sectoral Technical and Economic Cooperation (BIMSTEC) Secretary General Ambassador Indramani Pandey (left) with the author Col. Nalin Herath RSP\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s strategic location in the Indian Ocean has long been described as one of its greatest national assets. Yet geography alone does not create influence. It is the ability to translate geography into policy, partnerships, economic opportunity and regional leadership that ultimately determines how a country benefits from its strategic position. A recent conversation with the Bay of Bengal Initiative for Multi-Sectoral Technical and Economic Cooperation (BIMSTEC) Secretary General Ambassador Indramani Pandey reinforced this reality and highlighted an opportunity that Sri Lanka should not overlook.\n\nDuring his recent visit to Colombo, I had the privilege of meeting Ambassador Pandey and discussing the evolving role of BIMSTEC and the opportunities for greater engagement with Sri Lanka. What struck me was not only his understanding of the region but also his emphasis on practical cooperation in areas that are directly relevant to Sri Lanka\u2019s national interests.\n\nAmong the areas highlighted were maritime affairs and the blue economy, disaster mitigation, food security, counter-terrorism, anti-money laundering and combating human trafficking. These are not isolated subjects. Together, they represent some of the most important dimensions of regional security, economic resilience and sustainable development in the Bay of Bengal and the wider Indian Ocean. For Sri Lanka, maritime affairs should naturally occupy a central position in this agenda."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-14-dailyftlk-7e1f12b5",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7e1f12b5",
    "date_str": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "BIMSTEC, maritime Sri Lanka and the missing link in policy thinking",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/BIMSTEC-maritime-Sri-Lanka-and-the-missing-link-in-policy-thinking/4-797176",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1789329260.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailyftlk-7e1f12b5-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "044be1440834fc96fe237b21fe4e5a97",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1179,
    "chunk_text": "Among the areas highlighted were maritime affairs and the blue economy, disaster mitigation, food security, counter-terrorism, anti-money laundering and combating human trafficking. These are not isolated subjects. Together, they represent some of the most important dimensions of regional security, economic resilience and sustainable development in the Bay of Bengal and the wider Indian Ocean. For Sri Lanka, maritime affairs should naturally occupy a central position in this agenda.\n\nMy conversation with Ambassador Pandey took place shortly after the launch of the Ceylon Chamber of Shipping (CCS) at the Colombo International Maritime Conference. The Chamber was launched during the conference organised by the Colombo Shippers\u2019 Academy, founded by Rohan Masakorala. The launch was officiated by FMC Chairperson Laura DiBella and the Minister of Ports and Aviation.\n\nThe timing was significant. Sri Lanka needs stronger institutions that can bring together Government, the private sector, academia, professionals and international partners around maritime issues. A chamber dedicated to shipping and maritime affairs can become an important platform for such engagement."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-14-dailyftlk-7e1f12b5",
    "num": "dailyftlk-7e1f12b5",
    "date_str": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "BIMSTEC, maritime Sri Lanka and the missing link in policy thinking",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/BIMSTEC-maritime-Sri-Lanka-and-the-missing-link-in-policy-thinking/4-797176",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1789329260.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailyftlk-7e1f12b5-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d79e50f5703c943b1212bde2e6d2045b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2004,
    "chunk_text": "The timing was significant. Sri Lanka needs stronger institutions that can bring together Government, the private sector, academia, professionals and international partners around maritime issues. A chamber dedicated to shipping and maritime affairs can become an important platform for such engagement.\n\nI explained to Ambassador Pandey my continuing efforts to promote a stronger think-tank culture in Sri Lanka. Given my own background in the military, national security and policy research, I have increasingly come to believe that Sri Lanka needs institutions that can bridge the gap between political decision-making and academic research, institutions capable of producing practical, evidence-based policy recommendations.\n\nAmbassador Pandey\u2019s response was particularly encouraging. He emphasised that think tanks can be an important tool for influencing policymakers and helping governments implement sound policies. That observation deserves greater attention in Sri Lanka. We have no shortage of intelligent people, experienced professionals, academics or former policymakers. What we lack is a sufficiently developed ecosystem that systematically brings this expertise together and converts it into policy options. A healthy democracy needs political institutions, but it also needs institutions that can independently examine complex national and international issues, challenge conventional thinking and offer policymakers alternatives. Think tanks can perform precisely this function.\n\nThey can study geopolitical developments before they become crises. They can examine the economic implications of international agreements. They can develop options for maritime strategy, national security, energy security and food security. They can assess emerging technologies and their implications for national competitiveness. They can bring international experts into domestic policy debates and, equally importantly, provide Sri Lankan policymakers with access to international policy networks."
  },
