    "chunk_size_bytes": 1555,
    "chunk_text": "Since the drought-like weather began months ago, life in Kadurugaswewa village in Polonnaruwa district has become exceedingly difficult. The reservoir and wells have dried up, and vegetation has withered away leaving parched land and brittle plants. Now the villagers have to compete with wild animals for what little is left. The dry conditions that are causing this are linked to the ongoing effects of El Ni\u00f1o.\n\nShe said 30% of annual rainfall is usually gathered in the South-West monsoon period from May to September, but this time Sri Lanka received suppressed rainfall. When the South-West part receives some rain, the dry and intermediate zones are experiencing very dry conditions due to the ongoing El Ni\u00f1o. El Ni\u00f1o is one of the factors that can influence these rainfall patterns, although rainfall over Sri Lanka is affected by several other atmospheric and oceanic factors as well, She added.\n\n\u201cDuring October and November, there is potential for increased rainfall over the island, including episodes of heavy rainfall in some areas. Therefore, the possibility of localised flooding cannot be ruled out, particularly when heavy rainfall occurs over a short period,\u201d She added that rainfall and temperature conditions during the subsequent months could also vary from their usual patterns.\n\nHowever, during the North-East monsoon (December to February) and first inter monsoon period (March and April) that usually has few showers in the North Eastern part, will see a dryer than usual situation and people will feel high temperatures."
