    "chunk_text": "SLTDA Chairman Suranjith Wevita told Mirror Business the Pekoe Trail\u2019s route dictated its unique funding design, acting as a much-needed economic intervention for local councils that lack a formal tax base to manage waste in marginalized estate sectors.\n\nGovernance of the trail will rest with the SLTDA and other state entities, holding the larger share of directorships alongside private sector nominees on a structure drawn from long-distance trail management practices abroad. The Chairman confirmed the management entity is a non-profit organisation with no shareholders, meaning no dividends will be declared and all generated income will be used to develop the trail. Following other foreign donor agencies, the Asian Development Bank has signaled a willingness to fund further development and maintenance once the state stakeholders are fully on board.\n\nThe trail runs 300 kilometres across 22 stages through the Central Highlands, from south of Kandy through Hatton, Haputale, and Ella to Nuwara Eliya. It was developed under the Tourism Resilience Project with European Union funding and support from the US Agency for International Development, and has since been listed by Time among the World\u2019s Greatest Places in 2025 and by National Geographic among its top 20 travel experiences for 2024. The survey data underlines why the highlands are the primary testing ground for this pivot. Hiking and trekking has emerged as a top visitor draw, slightly edging out culinary tourism and surfing. Consequently, highland destinations are claiming a larger share of travel itineraries, with Ella now attracting more than half of all visitors, and Nuwara Eliya and Adam\u2019s Peak also seeing steady gains. However, capturing these tourists for longer stays remains a challenge. Visitors average just over a day in Nuwara Eliya and Adam\u2019s Peak, and a single day at Horton Plains, falling well short of the multi-day stays recorded at coastal spots such as Arugam Bay."
