    "md5": "c5d2abd868a7708e6bdd1446cbc0cd07",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1631,
    "chunk_text": "The controversy dates back to 2013, when SriLankan Airlines entered into arrangements involving Airbus A330-300 and A350-900 aircraft in a fleet-renewal programme valued over US$2.5 billion. For a struggling airline, commitments on that scale exposed vast public funds. The cancellation of some arrangements reportedly resulted in penalties exceeding US$100 million. Sri Lanka paid a heavy price for decisions made by custodians of a national asset.\n\nThose facts are serious, but another detail cannot be ignored: the 2020 UK judgment does not name Namal Rajapaksa. That does not prove his innocence, nor does it prevent investigators from obtaining evidence linking him to the matter. But the UK judgment itself cannot be presented as proof that Rajapaksa personally received the alleged bribe. This distinction separates a judicial finding from a political assertion. If the present case rests on evidence obtained after the UK proceedings, that evidence should be tested in court.\n\nFormer Board Director Professor Sonal Fernando has offered another perspective on aircraft procurement. He argued that commissions paid to buyers can be incorporated into aircraft pricing and claimed a transaction could become void if a buyer refused a seller\u2019s commission. He noted that aircraft can be purchased directly from manufacturers, through authorised representatives, or via brokers\u2014arguing that intermediaries can sometimes be commercially advantageous. Describing the international aircraft market as controlled by a \u201cmafia,\u201d Prof. Fernando criticised SriLankan Airlines\u2019 decision to pursue the A350 procurement as foolish."
