    "md5": "fa9c9db96d3c49b2eced7d042df80699",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1697,
    "chunk_text": "Following a change of government, SriLankan Airlines sought to withdraw from some aircraft arrangements, incurring penalties exceeding US$100 million. A high-powered inquiry followed, yet despite recommendations that former executives face prosecution, those investigations did not result in convictions. If corruption occurred, why did it take so long to pursue? If evidence existed, why did it fail to yield convictions? These questions cannot be answered simply by arresting another politician.\n\nThere is a legitimate public demand for accountability, but accountability without due process can become political vengeance. An arrest is not a conviction, an affidavit is not proof, and a foreign judgment that does not name an individual cannot be transformed into a finding against them. Conversely, the absence of a name from an earlier judgment should not shield anyone from legitimate new evidence. The correct path is neither automatic condemnation nor automatic exoneration; it is evidence. If investigators possess credible evidence linking Namal Rajapaksa to an illegal payment, they must present it in court.\n\nThe 2022 collapse was driven by weak institutions, policy failures, excessive borrowing, mismanagement, and corruption. Restoring public trust requires showing that rules apply equally to politicians, officials, businessmen, and state institutions alike. The Government must resist measuring success by high-profile arrests.\n\nIts real achievement would be building institutions strong enough that future governments cannot repeat past abuses. This focus on institutional reform is by no means an attempt to shield or clear corrupt politicians; accountability must be absolute."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-7dc8c2a0",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7dc8c2a0",
    "date_str": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "Political revenge or long overdue justice?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Political-revenge-or-long-overdue-justice/172-350250",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789368385.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-7dc8c2a0-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "216e3bf31f46791ef7a6bd7270bd0211",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Political revenge or long overdue justice?\n\nFor years, Sri Lankans watched politicians accused of corruption walk through the corridors of power while ordinary people struggled to survive. Now that investigations are reaching some of those who once enjoyed political power, the Opposition is crying foul.\n\nWhen allegations were made, where was this outrage? When questions were raised about public money, government contracts, questionable deals and the misuse of state resources, where was the concern for the people? Governments changed, politicians crossed the floor, and cases dragged on for years.\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake has taken a direct swipe at those who complain that the Government is targeting them politically. He has pointed to cases that remained unresolved for years and said that if they had been dealt with earlier, there would not be so many arrests taking place now. The President said fathers should have advised their sons not to steal. If a father had stopped his son when he first took a bribe, there would be no reason later to ask what could be done when that son was caught.\n\nSri Lanka has spent too many years normalising the idea that politicians can become extraordinarily wealthy while ordinary citizens are asked to tighten their belts. People stood in fuel queues. Families struggled with food prices. Businesses closed. Young people left the country looking for jobs. The country eventually went bankrupt. This is why the public has little sympathy when politicians being investigated suddenly discover the language of victimisation.\n\nIf you are innocent, go before the courts. If the evidence does not support the allegations, you will be cleared. An arrest is not a conviction. An investigation is not proof of guilt. Every suspect is entitled to due process and a fair hearing. But the opposite is also true. Calling every investigation \u201cpolitical revenge\u201d does not make an allegation disappear."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-7dc8c2a0",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-7dc8c2a0",
    "date_str": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "Political revenge or long overdue justice?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Political-revenge-or-long-overdue-justice/172-350250",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789368385.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-7dc8c2a0-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "045d2c52d83e0683379b4d4f790663cd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1882,
    "chunk_text": "If you are innocent, go before the courts. If the evidence does not support the allegations, you will be cleared. An arrest is not a conviction. An investigation is not proof of guilt. Every suspect is entitled to due process and a fair hearing. But the opposite is also true. Calling every investigation \u201cpolitical revenge\u201d does not make an allegation disappear.\n\nIf a former minister is accused of wrongdoing, investigate. If a former official is accused, investigate. If a businessman is involved, investigate. And if someone within the Government is accused of corruption, investigate them,\u00a0 too. The law cannot have one face for the Opposition and another for those in power. That is where the real test for President Dissanayake begins.\n\nIt is easy to promise a corruption-free country while sitting in Opposition. It is much harder to build institutions capable of investigating corruption without political interference. The question before the country is not whether politicians should be arrested. It is whether the institutions of the State are strong enough to investigate wrongdoing without fear or favour. Those entrusted with public money must be able to explain how it was used, and answer when questions arise.\n\nThe Opposition, instead of immediately claiming political victimisation whenever one of its members is investigated,\u00a0 should allow the legal process to take its course. If there is evidence, let the courts decide. If not, let the courts clear the accused. That is democracy. The people want one rule, the same rule for everyone.\n\nAnd perhaps that is the simplest lesson behind the President\u2019s comment about fathers and sons. Parents teach their children not to steal because stealing is wrong. A country must teach its politicians exactly the same lesson. Public money belongs to the public. A minister\u2019s office is not a personal bank account."
  },
