    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f56ac8db896a054f3f6392a60349e657",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "The document that we published, is not a traditional manifesto. It is a strategic plan. Here we have summarised our strategy if the country is to be taken to the next level. Our attempt was to establish the thought that the country needs a strategy. This is a document where we discuss strategies.\n\nQWhat is Sarvajana Balaya\u2019s long term objective? Is it to govern Sri Lanka long-term or to become a decisive power broker capable of determining which major party forms a government?\n\nWe need to govern the country, and we don\u2019t believe in becoming a power broker, because it will not solve the problems that the country has been facing for a long time. As Sarvajana Balaya we believe that we should build a national political narrative. But that must be done while protecting our civilizational values. We must build an entrepreneurial nation. In the short run, we want to influence every possible political party, the bureaucracy. This is the way forward. Finally, what we want is to implement this. To implement this, you must have power. But if you become a power broker, we will not be able to implement this strategy. Instead, we will become a party to a programme, which again is going to be very traditional and having a conventional model. Sarvajana Balaya is a progressive party that doesn\u2019t want to be party of a failed project.\n\nQIn 10 years\u2019 time, where would you position yourself and Sarvajana Balaya?\n\nIf all goes well, in 2029, we will have power. We must test our trajectory whether it has been convincing enough. So if people are convinced, we will then have another seven years. According to your question where we would be in ten years, we have expectations to implement our plans and show how competitive Sri Lanka will be in our region and also work towards people\u2019s happiness. We shall see."
  },
