    "md5": "8bd99f0d3b8ae431088dc5af875d7474",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "2016: the shock therapy model. When India abruptly demonetized its Rs. 500 and Rs. 1,000 notes overnight, roughly 86% of currency in circulation by value vanished from legal use with almost no warning.\n\n2023: the gradual withdrawal model. India\u2019s approach to phasing out its Rs. 2,000 note was the opposite in design. The Reserve Bank of India announced the withdrawal in May 2023 but kept the note as legal tender, gave the public a long window to deposit or exchange notes at bank branches, and kept RBI issue offices open for exchange well beyond the initial deadline even accepting notes by post. The result was an orderly, low-drama transition: by early 2026, roughly 98% of the withdrawn currency had been returned to the banking system, with no queues, no liquidity panic, and no measurable disruption to economic activity.\n\nNotably, this was also a case where the note being withdrawn was itself a high-value denomination introduced to remonetize the economy quickly after 2016, meaning India effectively used its own history to correct course, reducing the very anonymity-friendly denomination that shock therapy had reintroduced.\n\nThe takeaway for Sri Lanka is unambiguous. If the goal is to curb unaccounted cash without repeating India\u2019s 2016 trauma, the design should borrow directly from India\u2019s 2023 playbook a clear withdrawal announcement, an extended and well-publicized exchange window, continued legal-tender status during the transition, multiple deposit/exchange channels (including for those without easy access to bank branches), and no sudden invalidation of the note\u2019s value. The objective is not to punish cash holders overnight it is to make holding large sums outside the banking system progressively less convenient, while giving legitimate holders every reasonable opportunity to formalize their money."
