    "description": "The international audience doesn\u2019t understand the scale, brutality and complexity of Sri Lanka\u2019s armed conflict - Gen. Shavendra Silva",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/hard-talk/The-international-audience-doesnt-understand-the-scale-brutality-and-complexity-of-Sri-Lankas-armed-conflict-Gen-Shavendra-Silva/334-350328",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789454153.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-6149836b-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aaebc0f21014793988dd8a75a8bbff2b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1750,
    "chunk_text": "QThere are numerous attempts by LTTE sympathizers and propagandists who highlight atrocities committed by armed forces during the final stages of the war. What do you have to say about these narratives?\n\nQSome groups and individuals call the civil war a genocide. What are your comments about the use of the word genocide?\n\nWe feel sorry for those lives lost as a result of terrorism. The LTTE extensively used civilians as human shields and pioneered suicide terrorism long before many countries experienced similar threats. Sri Lanka\u2019s position has consistently been that many allegations remain politically contested and haven\u2019t been established through any judicial process or any international court of conviction. Sri Lanka had two or three mechanisms to investigate all types of allegations within the country according to the law of the land. Officers and soldiers who fought in the front lines including me were called and questioned about this. We have been questioned and clarified of our role and whatever the doubts were clarified then and there during the inquiry. However, I believe the more important question today is how societies prevent future cycles of terrorism, instability and state fragility. On May 21, 2009 while attending a ceremony near the abandoned Farah Ship at Vellamullivaikkal I told that no guns or bullets would be fired again on our soil due to terrorism. Still that statement is valid because terrorism in this country was eradicated. I\u2019m happy today to see the peace enjoyed by all Sri Lankans because terrorism is over in our country. Since 2009 Sri Lanka has focused on rehabilitation, reconstruction, reintegration and national recovery.\n\nQHow far have we come in terms of post-conflict stabilization?"
  },
