    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789454938.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-15-dailymirrorlk-09bdfa54-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7b71561581ed1aa09e08e94cab6e36e4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "Subsidy, price caps or hike? Govt. weighs fuel options\n\nColombo, Sep. 15 (Daily Mirror) - The government is considering several measures to address pressure on domestic fuel prices amid rising global crude oil costs, Power and Energy Minister Anura Karunathilaka has said.\n\nThe options include providing state fuel subsidies, similar to measures introduced in April and May, setting upper and lower price limits for private fuel distributors, or revising retail fuel prices.\n\nThe Minister said the government is assessing the available options before reaching a final decision.\n\n\u201cThe Middle East conflict has pushed global oil prices up, while domestic prices remain significantly lower. Several private fuel distributors say they cannot sustain operations under the current pricing structure, and the government is considering future measures,\u201d the Minister said.\n\nDuring periods of sharp price increases in April and May, the government provided fuel subsidies of Rs. 100 per litre for diesel and Rs. 20 per litre for petrol.\n\n\u201cTherefore, we may either adjust prices directly or allow CPC to sell at a lower price while setting upper and lower price limits for other fuel distributors,\u201d the minister said.\n\nAccording to the Minister, several private fuel companies have informed authorities that they are unable to sustain operations under the current pricing structure. The companies have reportedly cited losses ranging from Rs. 140 to Rs. 170 per litre from diesel and requested revisions to fuel prices.\n\n\u201cCompanies have submitted different figures, but no final decision has been made. Our priority remains ensuring consumers get fuel at fair, competitive prices,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Minister said the government also recognises the financial difficulties faced by private distributors while seeking to maintain competitive prices for consumers.\n\nHe said that the government's priority is to prevent a sudden financial burden on consumers while ensuring an uninterrupted fuel supply."
  },
