    "md5": "e99598d62ce08d6b1dcff492c72d61ed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1464,
    "chunk_text": "The Minister said the government also recognises the financial difficulties faced by private distributors while seeking to maintain competitive prices for consumers.\n\nHe said that the government's priority is to prevent a sudden financial burden on consumers while ensuring an uninterrupted fuel supply.\n\nSpeaking to the media, Rajakaruna said diesel pricing had emerged as a key challenge for the state-owned petroleum distributor, but said that the CPC was able to absorb the losses through profits generated from its refinery operations and the sale of jet fuel and other petroleum products.\n\nHe said the CPC\u2019s refinery operations had recently generated significant profits, providing the corporation with the financial capacity to offset losses incurred on diesel.\n\nThe Chairman also highlighted the CPC\u2019s competitive procurement process. According to Rajakaruna, the corporation obtains fuel through competitive tenders involving around 70 suppliers, helping it secure supplies at competitive prices and maintain tighter cost margins.\n\nRajakaruna also said there was no immediate need for a sudden fuel price increase. He explained that the fuel currently being sold had been ordered during the previous month, allowing the CPC to manage its costs under existing pricing conditions.\n\nDespite the losses on diesel, Rajakaruna maintained that the CPC remains capable of balancing its overall finances through revenue generated from its petroleum operations."
