    "md5": "29d94bdce3e85b3419fa44433d559564",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1912,
    "chunk_text": "No \u2018dog tax\u2019 under new Rabies Act: Vet\n\nColombo, Sep. 15 (Daily Mirror) - There is no new \u201cdog tax\u201d and no provision to jail people for feeding stray dogs under Sri Lanka\u2019s newly gazetted Rabies Eradication and Dog Registration Act, Veterinary Surgeon and Animal Welfare Coalition (AWC) Executive Member Dr. Chamith Nanayakkara said.\n\nSpeaking at a press conference, Dr. Nanayakkara said several claims circulating on social media about the new legislation were misleading and had created unnecessary public alarm.\n\nHe said dog registration was not a new requirement introduced by the Act, adding that local authorities already imposed nominal registration fees. The fee is currently Rs. 100 in the Colombo Municipal Council area and Rs. 300 in Anuradhapura, he said.\n\nDr. Nanayakkara said arrangements were being made to conduct dog registration alongside free rabies vaccination programmes conducted in villages, eliminating the need for owners to make separate visits to local authority offices.\n\nHe also clarified that feeding a stray dog does not automatically make a person its legal owner.\n\n\u201cFeeding a stray animal on a single occasion does not make a person its legal owner. However, if someone regularly feeds and interacts with an animal, there is a social responsibility to ensure that it receives necessary care, including rabies vaccination,\u201d he said.\n\nHe said genuine stray dogs with no caretaker would remain the responsibility of local authorities, which would be required to arrange vaccination and sterilisation.\n\nThe new legislation also introduces stricter provisions against the arbitrary killing of dogs, he said.\n\nAccording to Dr. Nanayakkara, the powers previously available under the 1894 Rabies Ordinance to kill animals found on the streets have been abolished.\n\n\u201cA dog cannot simply be killed because it has bitten someone or is suspected of having rabies,\u201d he said."
