    "md5": "4e50d6940d29fd77718225c6c1a136d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "It also highlights the importance of accreditation, workforce planning and stronger links between education, industry and professional practice. A well-planned system can provide young people with clearer opportunities while ensuring that the country develops the skills needed for future growth.\n\nThe second area is energy transition and power sector reform. The report finds that the main challenge is now implementation rather than absence of policy.\n\nThe sector must also balance energy security, reliability, affordability, financial sustainability and environmental responsibility. The report calls for better planning, stronger institutions, improved procurement, transparent tariffs, investor confidence and measurable performance.\n\nTransport is the third priority. The proposal is to establish an integrated multimodal people-centred public transport system that provides fast, affordable, reliable and seamless connectivity between cities, townships, economic centres and rural nodes.\n\nThe proposal considers an integrated fare and ticketing system, with the first and last-mile connectivity embedded into a national digital platform. The expected national-level benefits include reduced travel time, manageable congestion, reduced fuel consumption, lower logistics costs, enhanced productivity and improved regional connectivity.\n\nThe fourth area addresses building fire safety. Sri Lanka currently has fragmented responsibilities and there is no single national authority responsible for the continuous regulation of building fire safety.\n\nThe proposed framework calls for a National Building Fire Safety Act and an independent National Fire Safety Authority.\n\nIt also proposes regular inspections, certification, clear technical standards, professional review and effective enforcement. These measures are intended to protect lives and property and create consistent safety standards across the country."
